## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design a privacy-preserving ABAC layer that controls access to encrypted CCA score shares without revealing plaintext data. It tests understanding of dynamic admin access, revocation mechanisms, auditability, and system design at scale.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC attributes: tenant_id, region, role, expiry
- Token-based access without decryption
- Immutable audit logs with tamper-evident provenance
- Key management with per-tenant keys
- Revocation and expiry propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluatePolicy(attrs, action) {
  const now = Date.now();

  if (attrs.expiry < now) return { allowed: false, reason: 'expired' };
  if (!attrs.tenant_id || !attrs.region) return { allowed: false, reason: 'missing attributes' };

  const policy = getPolicy(attrs.policy_id);
  if (!policy) return { allowed: false, reason: 'policy not found' };

  const regionMatch = policy.regions.includes(attrs.region);
  const roleMatch = policy.roles.includes(attrs.role);

  return { allowed: regionMatch && roleMatch, reason: regionMatch && roleMatch ? 'ok' : 'attribute mismatch' };
}

function revokeAccess(policyId, reason) {
  const policy = getPolicy(policyId);
  policy.revoked = true;
  policy.revocationReason = reason;
  publishRevocationEvent(policyId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This checks understanding of IND-CCA security and how to build practical, protocol-level defenses in modern cryptographic systems.

## Key Concepts

- IND-CCA and chosen-ciphertext resistance
- Encrypt-then-MAC vs MAC-then-Encrypt
- Nonce management, associated data, and MAC verification order
- Trade-offs: performance, ciphertext size, API surface

## Code Example

```javascript
function encrypt(plaintext, key) {
  const nonce = crypto.randomBytes(12);
  const cipher = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-128-gcm', key, nonce);
  const enc = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);
  const tag = cipher.getAuthTag();
  const payload = Buffer.concat([nonce, enc, tag]);
  const mac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key).update(payload).digest();
  return { payload, mac };
}

function decrypt(payload, mac, key) {
  if (!verifyMac(mac, payload, key)) throw new Error('MAC fail');
  const nonce = payload.slice(0, 12);
  const enc = payload.slice(12, -16);
  const tag = payload.slice(-16);
  const decipher = crypto.createDecipheriv('aes-128-gcm', key, nonce);
  decipher.setAuthTag(tag);
  return Buffer.concat([decipher.update(enc), decipher.final()]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you audit for decryption oracle leakage?
- How would you enforce constant-time MAC checks and retry limits to prevent side channels?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design forward- and backward-compatible APIs—a common real-world constraint during model upgrades.

## Key Concepts
- Version negotiation and routing
- Compatibility shim and input mapping
- Migration plan, deprecation notices, and rollback strategy
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Observability: parity checks and rollback triggers

## Implementation Approach
Implement version-aware routing in /cca_score/compute. Maintain a lightweight compatibility shim that maps v1 inputs to v2 scoring logic, ensuring output parity where possible. Include migration_status fields to track client version transitions, and add deprecation_notice in responses for outdated clients. Use model_version headers or query parameters for routing decisions, with graceful defaults when clients omit version information. The config endpoint (POST /cca_score/config) returns the latest supported version and feature flags, enabling clients to self-upgrade. Store client_timestamp to enable time-based analysis of migration progress.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo mapping: v1 -> v2 input schema
function mapV1toV2(v1) {
  return {
    userId: v1.user_id,
    locale: v1.locale,
    region: v1.region,
    modelVersion: 'v2',
    clientTs: v1.client_timestamp
  };
}

// Version-aware routing
function routeRequest(req) {
  const version = req.headers['x-model-version'] || 'v1';
  if (version === 'v1') return handleV1(mapV1toV2(req.body));
  return handleV2(req.body);
}
```
